2. STUDY OBJECTIVES & ANALYTICAL QUESTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.1 Primary Objective:
To evaluate the empirical relationships among IP literacy, professional innovation 
attitudes, perceived institutional readiness, and reported innovation practice 
participation among pharmacy personnel in post-conflict Syria (N = 303).

2.2 Core Analytical Questions:
- Question 1: Is high attitudinal endorsement of innovation translated into active 
  practice participation, or does a significant implementation gap exist?
- Question 2: Do individual knowledge, attitudes, or perceived institutional readiness 
  statistically predict innovation practice participation when controlling for 
  demographic and professional sectors?
- Question 3: Are there significant differences in innovation practice participation 
  across professional sectors (undergraduate students, academic researchers, 
  practicing pharmacists)?

3. SAMPLE SPECIFICATION & DEMOGRAPHICS (N = 303)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 Sample Size & Complete Case Analysis:
A total of 303 verified complete responses obtained from 400 distributed invitations 
(75.75% response rate). Single-response restriction enforced via IP and browser tracking. 
Missing values: 0 (100% complete case analysis).

3.2 Sample Stratification:
- Gender: Female (Gender=1) = 215 (70.96%), Male (Gender=0) = 88 (29.04%).
- Professional Sector: Undergraduate Pharmacy Students = 224 (73.93%), 
  Academic/Graduate Researchers = 54 (17.82%), Practicing Pharmacists = 25 (8.25%).
- Experience Level: <1 Year = 80 (26.40%), 1–5 Years = 153 (50.50%), 
  6–10 Years = 45 (14.85%), >10 Years = 25 (8.25%).

4. VARIABLE OPERATIONALIZATION (FORMATIVE COMPOSITE INDICES)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All core constructs are operationalized as Formative Composite Indices (FCIs) 
normalized to a 0–100 scale:
1. PILS_pct: IP Literacy Score (9 binary objective legal items, K2–K10).
2. IAI_pct: Attitudinal Endorsement Index (10 Likert items, A1–A10, reverse coding for A2, A5).
3. IRS_pct: Perceived Institutional Readiness Score (4 objective binary items on TTOs, legal support, funding, R&D infrastructure, R1–R4).
4. IPI_pct: Innovation Practice Participation Score (3 behavioral binary items on patents, R&D, tech transfer, P1–P3).
5. Diagnostic_Gap: Implementation Diagnostic Gap arithmetic difference (IAI_pct - IPI_pct).

5. STATISTICAL METHODOLOGY & ANALYTICAL PIPELINE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.1 Software & Environment:
Python 3.12 utilizing Pandas, NumPy, SciPy, and Statsmodels packages.

5.2 Descriptive Statistics & Zero-Inflation Diagnostics:
Calculation of Mean, SD, Median, IQR, Min, Max, Skewness, and exact count/percentage 
of zero scores to quantify floor effects.

5.3 Sectoral Group Comparisons:
Primary Non-parametric Test: Kruskal-Wallis H-test across professional sectors 
(Students, Researchers, Pharmacists).
Secondary Parametric Test: One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) F-test.

5.4 Bivariate Correlation Analysis:
Non-parametric Spearman rank correlation matrix (r_s) with exact p-values across 
core domains (PILS, IAI, IRS, IPI).

5.5 Multivariable Predictive Modeling (Handling Zero-Inflation):
- Stage 1 Primary Model: Binary Logistic Regression predicting any practice 
  participation (IPI > 0) with Odds Ratios (95% CI) and p-values.
- Stage 2 Secondary Model: Binomial Generalized Linear Model (GLM) modeling 
  practice density via a joint (Successes, Failures) count vector based on the 3 
  binary indicators, with Odds Ratios (95% CI) and p-values.

